1️⃣ Build the Full Stack Application
Create a simple app:

/fullstack-app
   ├── client/        # React frontend
   ├── server/        # Node.js + Express backend
2️⃣ Prepare AWS Environment
Create a VPC (Virtual Private Cloud)

Include public and private subnets

Configure Internet Gateway for public access

Set Up Security Groups

Frontend EC2 Security Group: Allow HTTP (80) and SSH (22)

Backend EC2 Security Group: Allow HTTP (3000) and SSH (22); limit inbound to ALB’s security group

3️⃣ Launch EC2 Instances
Frontend Instance:

AMI: Amazon Linux 2 or Ubuntu

Install Node.js and Nginx

Serve the React app via Nginx

bash
Copy code
sudo yum install -y nginx
sudo systemctl start nginx
sudo systemctl enable nginx
Copy the React build files into /usr/share/nginx/html

Backend Instances (2 or more):

Install Node.js and your backend code

bash
Copy code
git clone https://github.com/<your-repo>/server.git
cd server
npm install
node index.js
Ensure your backend runs on port 3000

4️⃣ Set Up Load Balancer
Navigate to EC2 → Load Balancers → Create Load Balancer

Choose Application Load Balancer (ALB)

Configure:

Listeners: HTTP on port 80

Availability Zones: Select multiple for redundancy

Target Group: Add your backend EC2 instances (port 3000)

Register both backend instances to the target group.

5️⃣ Test Load Balancing
Access the Load Balancer DNS name (shown in AWS console)

Try refreshing multiple times — requests should be distributed between backend servers (you can verify with unique console logs or instance IDs per server).

6️⃣ (Optional) Set Up Route 53 Domain
Purchase or connect your domain in AWS Route 53

Create a new A Record pointing to your Load Balancer DNS

Type: A

Alias: Yes → Choose your ALB name

Your app will now be accessible via your domain, e.g.
👉 https://yourdomain.com
